@page "/getting-started"

<PageHeader Kicker="Introduction" Title="Getting started">
    One package, one registration and a context factory - then EF Core runs against a SQLite database in the
    user's browser tab, on the storage backend your preference order picks. Every page after this one runs
    against that database, in this tab.
</PageHeader>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Install</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        <code>Bit.Bsqlite</code> is the whole install: the ADO.NET surface, the EF Core integration, the worker
        transport and every storage backend - OPFS, IndexedDB, Cache Storage and in-memory. It targets .NET 10 and
        11, each with the EF Core of the same major version. Browser-free unit tests add
        <code>Bit.Bsqlite.Testing</code> to the test project.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock Title="Terminal" Language="shell" Code="@Snippets.Start.Install" Collapsible="false" />
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Register the services</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Register the backends you ship, the order you prefer them in, and the database. The first available backend
        wins, but existing data outranks preference: a database created on IndexedDB stays there once OPFS becomes
        available. <code>AllowNonPersistentFallback()</code> is what makes in-memory acceptable at all.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock Title="Program.cs" Language="csharp" Code="@Snippets.Start.Registration" Collapsible="false" />
    <div class="bb-note">
        In a Blazor Web App whose pages are prerendered, register the same services in the server project too: the
        prerender pass instantiates the pages there, so what they inject has to resolve. Nothing touches the browser
        until a page opens the database. This site keeps both registrations in one <code>AddDemoServices</code>
        extension method that both Program.cs files call.
    </div>
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Open a context and query</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Inject the context factory wherever you would have opened a <code>DbContext</code>. The first context opens
        the database and runs the migrations once per session; later ones reuse the session's connection. From there
        it is ordinary EF Core - with the async API throughout, because the engine runs in a worker the UI thread
        cannot wait on.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock Title="Pages/Products.razor" Language="razor" Code="@Snippets.Start.Page" Collapsible="false" />
    <div class="bb-note bb-note-warn">
        Synchronous members - <code>SaveChanges()</code>, <code>ToList()</code>, <code>DbConnection.Open()</code> -
        throw <code>BsqliteSynchronousApiNotSupportedException</code> naming the async overload. The
        <a href="limits">Limits</a> page runs that on purpose.
    </div>
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Prerendering and render modes</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        A prerendered page runs once on the server, where there is no browser to open a database in. Open it only once
        the page is interactive - <code>RendererInfo.IsInteractive</code> on Interactive WebAssembly, and
        <code>OperatingSystem.IsBrowser()</code> on Interactive Auto, where a page on the server circuit is
        interactive too. This site renders <code>&lt;Routes&gt;</code> with InteractiveWebAssembly and lets its
        <code>SessionLifecycle</code> component do the check for every page.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock Title="A page that is prerendered" Language="csharp" Code="@Snippets.Start.Prerendered" Collapsible="false" />
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Interactive Server is not supported: a user's database lives in their browser, and Bsqlite will not keep one
        file on the server for every user. A Blazor Hybrid app - MAUI, WPF, Windows Forms - is supported with the same
        registration: its database is a SQLite file on the device, on the <code>file-system</code> backend, which is
        registered and selected for it automatically.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Lazy loading on WebAssembly</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        EF Core and Bsqlite are most of what a WebAssembly app downloads for its database - about 2.1 MB, compressed
        with Brotli. An app whose database only some pages use can mark them <code>BlazorWebAssemblyLazyLoad</code>,
        load them from the router's <code>OnNavigateAsync</code> before those pages render, and leave the registration
        out of <code>Program.cs</code>: <code>GetBitBsqliteServices</code> makes the same registration the first time a
        page asks in the browser, once for the app, from the one method every page passes it, and still reaches the
        app's own services.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock Title="AppDatabase.cs" Language="csharp" Code="@Snippets.Start.LazyServices" Collapsible="false" />
    <CodeBlock Title="App.razor" Language="razor" Code="@Snippets.Start.LazyRouter" Collapsible="false" />
    <div class="bb-note bb-note-warn">
        Keep EF Core's and Bsqlite's types out of everything that runs before the load, and keep the context and its
        migrations out of the assembly the router reads - in a library lazy loaded with the rest, as this site's Data
        project is - or the app fails on its first page when it is opened away from the database. AOT builds do not
        lazy load. The assemblies to mark and every rule are in the guide's
        <a href="https://github.com/bitfoundation/bitplatform/blob/develop/src/Utilities/Bsqlite/docs/getting-started.md#lazy-loading-on-webassembly" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Lazy loading on WebAssembly</a>.
    </div>
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Nothing in the host page</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        The engine (<code>.wasm</code>), the worker and the backends' VFSes are static web assets under
        <code>_content/Bit.Bsqlite/</code>, each imported on demand: registering a backend costs nothing until it is
        selected, and a session downloads one engine build and the VFS of the backend it selected. There is no script
        tag or stylesheet to add.
    </p>
</section>

